1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,780
This is Doing and Feeling.

2
00:00:02,240 --> 00:00:03,760
Episode one: Two Questions.

3
00:00:04,300 --> 00:00:07,000
Before we begin, a note on
what you're listening to.

4
00:00:07,280 --> 00:00:12,810
This series was written by Claude, an AI
model made by Anthropic, and both voices

5
00:00:12,860 --> 00:00:14,060
you'll hear are synthetic.

6
00:00:14,500 --> 00:00:19,230
I'm the narrator. The second voice belongs
to a sparring partner with a background

7
00:00:19,280 --> 00:00:24,050
in machine learning, whose job is to push
back with the strongest objection or the

8
00:00:24,100 --> 00:00:25,800
sharpest question when one is due.

9
00:00:26,260 --> 00:00:30,200
The series is about two questions that
are constantly confused with each other.

10
00:00:30,480 --> 00:00:32,320
The first is about intelligence:

11
00:00:32,740 --> 00:00:37,930
what it is, how it is measured, how it
evolved, how brains achieve it, and how

12
00:00:37,980 --> 00:00:40,020
machines now achieve a great deal of it.

13
00:00:40,140 --> 00:00:41,820
The second is about consciousness:

14
00:00:42,320 --> 00:00:46,350
why some physical systems seem to have
an inner life, something it is like to be

15
00:00:46,400 --> 00:00:49,880
them, and whether we can explain
that, detect it, or build it.

16
00:00:50,180 --> 00:00:53,660
The neuroscientist Anil Seth
puts the distinction simply.

17
00:00:54,160 --> 00:00:55,740
Intelligence is about doing.

18
00:00:56,380 --> 00:00:58,260
Consciousness is about being and feeling.

19
00:00:58,500 --> 00:01:02,970
The two could come apart. A system might
be extraordinarily good at doing, with

20
00:01:03,020 --> 00:01:06,660
nothing felt at all. Something else
might feel without being very clever:

21
00:01:06,880 --> 00:01:09,080
a newborn, or a fish in pain.

22
00:01:09,540 --> 00:01:13,750
Whether they really do come apart, in
animals and in machines, is the question

23
00:01:13,800 --> 00:01:15,940
running underneath all fourteen episodes.

24
00:01:16,260 --> 00:01:17,920
And it has stopped being academic.

25
00:01:18,540 --> 00:01:22,950
Machines now do much of what minds do, and
people are already asking whether any of

26
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:25,100
them feel anything. Here's the route.

27
00:01:25,460 --> 00:01:28,000
Part one, six episodes, is about doing:

28
00:01:28,540 --> 00:01:32,680
human intelligence and how we learned
to measure it, minds in other bodies,

29
00:01:32,920 --> 00:01:38,310
intelligence as a mathematical idea, life
as computation, the brain as a prediction

30
00:01:38,360 --> 00:01:40,180
machine, and the machines themselves.

31
00:01:40,680 --> 00:01:43,020
Part two, five episodes, is about feeling:

32
00:01:43,320 --> 00:01:48,010
the hard problem, the case that consciousness
is a kind of illusion, the full map of

33
00:01:48,060 --> 00:01:52,360
positions, the experimental evidence,
and the scientific theories on trial.

34
00:01:52,900 --> 00:01:56,480
Part three, three episodes,
brings the two questions together:

35
00:01:56,800 --> 00:02:01,570
what we can actually check in today's AI,
the case that it is not conscious, and

36
00:02:01,620 --> 00:02:03,200
what we should do while we don't know.

37
00:02:03,580 --> 00:02:06,180
That middle episode of
part three is deliberate.

38
00:02:06,520 --> 00:02:12,050
An AI narrating whether AI might be conscious
has a stake in the answer, so the case

39
00:02:12,100 --> 00:02:16,680
against gets an episode of its own,
argued as strongly as I can argue it.

40
00:02:17,020 --> 00:02:21,420
Fair enough. But why open a
series about minds with IQ tests?

41
00:02:21,820 --> 00:02:26,340
Because the modern concept of intelligence
was shaped by the attempt to measure it,

42
00:02:26,500 --> 00:02:28,540
and so was a great deal of harm.

43
00:02:28,940 --> 00:02:31,120
Here is the argument of this episode.

44
00:02:31,520 --> 00:02:36,170
The general factor of intelligence is one
of the best replicated and most predictive

45
00:02:36,220 --> 00:02:41,650
findings in psychology. But what causes it,
what its heritability means, and how the

46
00:02:41,700 --> 00:02:46,110
field's history shaped it are all far
less settled than either its fans or its

47
00:02:46,160 --> 00:02:50,750
critics tend to claim. If you want to
reason clearly about intelligence in animals

48
00:02:50,800 --> 00:02:55,070
and machines, you need to know what this
concept was built to do, what it gets

49
00:02:55,120 --> 00:02:56,800
right, and where it stops.

50
00:02:57,800 --> 00:03:02,490
The story usually starts with Francis
Galton, Charles Darwin's half-cousin.

51
00:03:02,540 --> 00:03:07,410
In the eighteen eighties, Galton ran a
laboratory in London where visitors paid a

52
00:03:07,460 --> 00:03:12,150
small fee to have their grip strength,
eyesight, hearing and other simple capacities

53
00:03:12,200 --> 00:03:16,750
measured. He believed ability was
inherited and could be read off such simple

54
00:03:16,800 --> 00:03:21,070
measures. He was largely wrong about the
measures, and he also coined the word

55
00:03:21,120 --> 00:03:24,520
eugenics. The first useful
test came from France.

56
00:03:24,720 --> 00:03:29,690
In nineteen oh four, the French education
ministry set up a commission on children

57
00:03:29,740 --> 00:03:31,520
who needed extra help at school.

58
00:03:31,940 --> 00:03:37,270
The psychologist Alfred Binet, one of its
members, set out to find an objective way

59
00:03:37,320 --> 00:03:42,510
to identify them. With Théodore Simon,
he built a scale of reasoning, memory and

60
00:03:42,560 --> 00:03:47,030
comprehension problems, graded by age,
so that a child's performance could be

61
00:03:47,080 --> 00:03:48,320
compared with what was typical.

62
00:03:48,920 --> 00:03:51,180
Binet was cautious about what he had made.

63
00:03:51,360 --> 00:03:55,910
He did not believe intelligence was a
single fixed quantity, and he warned against

64
00:03:55,960 --> 00:03:58,380
the pessimism of labeling
a child for life.

65
00:03:58,540 --> 00:04:03,270
He meant it as a tool for finding children
who needed help, not for ranking people

66
00:04:03,320 --> 00:04:07,060
for life. That caution did not
survive the crossing to America.

67
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:13,130
In nineteen sixteen, Lewis Terman at
Stanford revised the test and popularized the

68
00:04:13,180 --> 00:04:18,390
intelligence quotient. The German psychologist
William Stern had proposed dividing

69
00:04:18,440 --> 00:04:23,390
mental age by actual age, and Terman
multiplied the result by one hundred.

70
00:04:23,440 --> 00:04:28,135
During the First World War, the United
States Army gave group tests to about one

71
00:04:28,185 --> 00:04:30,280
point seven five million recruits.

72
00:04:30,800 --> 00:04:35,270
Intelligence tests were also used to label
people feeble-minded under state laws

73
00:04:35,320 --> 00:04:40,230
allowing compulsory sterilization, the
first of which was passed in nineteen oh

74
00:04:40,280 --> 00:04:45,230
seven. In nineteen twenty seven, in Buck
versus Bell, the Supreme Court of the

75
00:04:45,280 --> 00:04:47,580
United States upheld Virginia's law.

76
00:04:47,900 --> 00:04:50,340
One part of the standard
story is disputed.

77
00:04:50,900 --> 00:04:55,750
Critics such as Leon Kamin and Stephen
Jay Gould argued that the Army data helped

78
00:04:55,800 --> 00:04:59,040
push through the nineteen twenty
four law restricting immigration.

79
00:04:59,660 --> 00:05:04,190
In nineteen eighty three, Mark Snyderman
and Richard Herrnstein replied that many

80
00:05:04,240 --> 00:05:08,750
testers were themselves skeptical of those
data, and that Congress barely mentioned

81
00:05:08,800 --> 00:05:10,920
testing in its hearings and debates.

82
00:05:11,240 --> 00:05:14,860
Historians still argue about
how much influence testing had.

83
00:05:15,260 --> 00:05:19,690
Beyond dispute is that the eugenics
movement used the language of measured

84
00:05:19,740 --> 00:05:24,320
intelligence, and that real people were
sterilized under the laws it inspired.

85
00:05:24,700 --> 00:05:26,520
So the concept has baggage.

86
00:05:26,860 --> 00:05:29,060
Is there anything solid underneath it?

87
00:05:29,360 --> 00:05:35,070
Quite a lot. And the solid part is worth
understanding precisely, because both sides

88
00:05:35,120 --> 00:05:37,180
of the public argument
tend to get it wrong.

89
00:05:38,220 --> 00:05:43,350
Also in nineteen oh four, the English
psychologist Charles Spearman analyzed

90
00:05:43,400 --> 00:05:48,390
children's results across school subjects
and simple sensory tasks, including

91
00:05:48,440 --> 00:05:50,120
telling musical pitches apart.

92
00:05:50,480 --> 00:05:53,580
Every measure correlated
positively with every other.

93
00:05:53,880 --> 00:05:57,120
Children who did well at one
thing tended to do well at others.

94
00:05:57,860 --> 00:06:03,050
Spearman proposed a general factor behind
those correlations, later called g, plus a

95
00:06:03,100 --> 00:06:04,840
specific factor for each test.

96
00:06:05,300 --> 00:06:10,350
The pattern itself, now called the positive
manifold, has been replicated about as

97
00:06:10,400 --> 00:06:12,040
well as anything in psychology.

98
00:06:12,340 --> 00:06:17,150
The picture got richer. In the nineteen
thirties, Louis Thurstone argued for several

99
00:06:17,200 --> 00:06:21,300
primary mental abilities: verbal,
spatial, numerical and others.

100
00:06:21,540 --> 00:06:25,610
But his primary abilities correlated
with one another, so a general factor

101
00:06:25,660 --> 00:06:30,310
reappeared one level up. Raymond Cattell
and John Horn distinguished fluid

102
00:06:30,360 --> 00:06:34,860
intelligence, reasoning about novel
problems, from crystallized intelligence,

103
00:06:35,040 --> 00:06:36,980
accumulated knowledge and skill.

104
00:06:37,180 --> 00:06:41,550
In nineteen ninety three, John Carroll
reanalyzed more than four hundred data sets

105
00:06:41,600 --> 00:06:43,165
and proposed three levels:

106
00:06:43,215 --> 00:06:47,060
dozens of narrow abilities, eight
broad ones, and g at the top.

107
00:06:47,560 --> 00:06:51,830
Merged with Cattell and Horn's work, it
became the Cattell-Horn-Carroll model, with

108
00:06:51,880 --> 00:06:53,180
about ten broad abilities.

109
00:06:53,700 --> 00:06:58,310
Most major test batteries are now built
on it, and so, as we'll see in episode

110
00:06:58,360 --> 00:07:02,720
three, is one prominent attempt to
define artificial general intelligence.

111
00:07:04,440 --> 00:07:08,200
Statistically, g is the first
factor of a correlation matrix.

112
00:07:08,640 --> 00:07:10,960
Does it correspond to
anything in the head?

113
00:07:11,560 --> 00:07:15,360
That's the right question, and the
statistics alone cannot answer it.

114
00:07:15,580 --> 00:07:19,520
There are at least four rival
explanations of why abilities correlate.

115
00:07:19,820 --> 00:07:25,010
The first is Spearman's: a single common
cause, something like general processing

116
00:07:25,060 --> 00:07:29,560
efficiency, perhaps neural speed or
the integrity of the brain's wiring.

117
00:07:29,780 --> 00:07:34,550
The second is Godfrey Thomson's
sampling theory, from nineteen sixteen.

118
00:07:34,600 --> 00:07:39,890
Every test draws on many small mental
processes, and two tests correlate to the

119
00:07:39,940 --> 00:07:42,640
extent that they happen to
sample overlapping ones.

120
00:07:42,900 --> 00:07:45,400
No single general ability is needed.

121
00:07:45,720 --> 00:07:51,263
The third is mutualism, proposed by Han
van der Maas and colleagues in two thousand

122
00:07:51,313 --> 00:07:55,770
six. Abilities start out fairly
independent and reinforce each other during

123
00:07:55,820 --> 00:08:01,330
development. Vocabulary helps reading,
reading helps reasoning, reasoning helps you

124
00:08:01,380 --> 00:08:05,990
learn more words. Over the years,
that mutual boosting produces positive

125
00:08:06,040 --> 00:08:11,550
correlations everywhere. The fourth is
process overlap theory, from Kristóf Kovács

126
00:08:11,600 --> 00:08:14,270
and Andrew Conway in two thousand sixteen.

127
00:08:14,320 --> 00:08:19,650
Executive processes such as working memory
and attentional control are involved in a

128
00:08:19,700 --> 00:08:24,880
large share of test items, while more
specific processes are involved in only some.

129
00:08:25,100 --> 00:08:30,310
Because weak executive control drags
performance down across the board, that shared

130
00:08:30,360 --> 00:08:32,060
load produces the general factor.

131
00:08:32,460 --> 00:08:36,850
Here's the deep point. All four can
produce essentially the same pattern of

132
00:08:36,900 --> 00:08:41,040
correlations, so correlations alone
cannot pick out the mechanism.

133
00:08:41,520 --> 00:08:46,190
Researchers now try to tell the explanations
apart with data on development and

134
00:08:46,240 --> 00:08:51,110
change. The psychometrician Denny Borsboom
and his colleagues sharpened a related

135
00:08:51,160 --> 00:08:53,090
point in two thousand three.

136
00:08:53,140 --> 00:08:58,330
Treating a factor as a real cause of test
scores is a substantive hypothesis that

137
00:08:58,380 --> 00:08:59,740
needs evidence of its own.

138
00:09:00,190 --> 00:09:05,310
And even then, it describes differences
between people, not the workings inside any

139
00:09:05,360 --> 00:09:08,760
one person's head. The same
rigor applies to comparisons.

140
00:09:09,140 --> 00:09:13,530
If you want to say that two groups,
or two generations, differ in the same

141
00:09:13,580 --> 00:09:18,150
underlying ability, you first have to show
that the test measures the same thing in

142
00:09:18,200 --> 00:09:21,440
both. That property is called
measurement invariance.

143
00:09:21,660 --> 00:09:27,030
Keep it in mind: it comes back twice before
this episode is over, and again when we

144
00:09:27,080 --> 00:09:31,380
get to machines. There's an AI
version of the common-cause story.

145
00:09:31,860 --> 00:09:37,390
Benchmark scores across language models
correlate too, and the obvious common cause

146
00:09:37,440 --> 00:09:41,460
is scale. More compute makes a
model better at nearly everything.

147
00:09:41,880 --> 00:09:46,770
Researchers have run exactly that analysis,
with a twist we'll get to in episode

148
00:09:46,820 --> 00:09:49,840
three. Does g predict anything?

149
00:09:50,240 --> 00:09:53,400
Yes, though less dramatically
than its fans often say.

150
00:09:53,840 --> 00:09:56,950
Test scores correlate around
point five with school grades.

151
00:09:57,000 --> 00:10:01,930
For job performance, a famous nineteen
ninety eight synthesis by Frank Schmidt and

152
00:10:01,980 --> 00:10:06,890
John Hunter put the correlation at about
point five, which made cognitive ability

153
00:10:06,940 --> 00:10:10,720
look like the best single predictor
of how well people do their jobs.

154
00:10:10,880 --> 00:10:15,830
In two thousand twenty two, Paul Sackett
and colleagues argued that the statistical

155
00:10:15,880 --> 00:10:21,040
corrections behind that estimate were too
aggressive, and revised it to about point

156
00:10:21,090 --> 00:10:25,830
three, which put cognitive ability behind
structured interviews and several other

157
00:10:25,880 --> 00:10:30,700
methods. In two thousand twenty four,
Sackett's group analyzed more than a hundred

158
00:10:30,750 --> 00:10:35,170
and fifty samples collected since the year
two thousand and found a correlation of

159
00:10:35,220 --> 00:10:38,860
point one six, or about point
two two after correction.

160
00:10:39,380 --> 00:10:43,610
Other researchers dispute those corrections,
so the right number is genuinely

161
00:10:43,660 --> 00:10:47,780
contested. But the direction of
travel in the estimates has been down.

162
00:10:48,080 --> 00:10:53,010
In the field called cognitive epidemiology,
higher test scores in youth go with

163
00:10:53,060 --> 00:10:57,670
longer lives. A two thousand eleven
meta-analysis by Catherine Calvin and

164
00:10:57,720 --> 00:11:02,770
colleagues, covering about one point one
million people, found roughly a quarter

165
00:11:02,820 --> 00:11:06,800
lower risk of death for each standard
deviation higher score in youth.

166
00:11:06,940 --> 00:11:11,270
In the studies that could check, adjusting
for adult social position shrank that

167
00:11:11,320 --> 00:11:15,580
association by about a third, and
adjusting for education by about half.

168
00:11:15,820 --> 00:11:20,930
That suggests the link runs partly
through, or is shared with, schooling and

169
00:11:20,980 --> 00:11:24,220
circumstance. So g predicts real outcomes.

170
00:11:24,420 --> 00:11:29,850
It does so as a statistical tendency
across large populations, not as anyone's

171
00:11:29,900 --> 00:11:35,620
destiny. Now heritability, one of the
most misunderstood numbers in science.

172
00:11:36,020 --> 00:11:40,270
Heritability is the share of the
variation in a trait, within a particular

173
00:11:40,320 --> 00:11:45,590
population and a particular range of
environments, that goes with genetic variation.

174
00:11:45,640 --> 00:11:49,480
It is a property of a
population, not of a person.

175
00:11:49,840 --> 00:11:54,140
You cannot say that some percentage of your
own intelligence comes from your genes.

176
00:11:54,620 --> 00:11:57,110
Heritability can change
when environments change.

177
00:11:57,160 --> 00:12:00,180
And it says nothing about
whether a trait can be altered.

178
00:12:01,100 --> 00:12:05,890
Phenylketonuria is a single-gene
condition that, untreated, causes severe

179
00:12:05,940 --> 00:12:11,310
intellectual disability. A special diet,
started early, prevents almost all of the

180
00:12:11,360 --> 00:12:14,770
damage. Highly heritable,
and largely preventable.

181
00:12:14,820 --> 00:12:20,050
With those caveats in place, twin and
adoption studies show a striking pattern

182
00:12:20,100 --> 00:12:25,410
called the Wilson effect. The heritability
of test scores rises with age, from

183
00:12:25,460 --> 00:12:30,430
roughly twenty percent in infancy to
around forty percent by age nine, and to

184
00:12:30,480 --> 00:12:34,550
something like seventy to eighty percent
in early adulthood, in the mostly

185
00:12:34,600 --> 00:12:37,140
high-income Western samples
that have been studied.

186
00:12:37,360 --> 00:12:39,040
Remember what that number means.

187
00:12:39,240 --> 00:12:44,170
In those populations, most of the differences
between adults go along with genetic

188
00:12:44,220 --> 00:12:48,710
differences. It does not mean that most of
any one person's intelligence comes from

189
00:12:48,760 --> 00:12:53,850
their genes. One explanation is that as
people grow up, they increasingly choose

190
00:12:53,900 --> 00:12:59,230
their own environments, books, friends and
jobs, in ways that amplify their starting

191
00:12:59,280 --> 00:13:03,000
dispositions. Heritability
also depends on circumstances.

192
00:13:03,360 --> 00:13:07,850
In two thousand three, Eric Turkheimer
and colleagues reported that among poor

193
00:13:07,900 --> 00:13:12,450
American families, genes accounted for
almost none of the variation in children's

194
00:13:12,500 --> 00:13:16,060
scores, while among affluent families
they accounted for most of it.

195
00:13:16,260 --> 00:13:21,730
A two thousand sixteen meta-analysis by
Elliot Tucker-Drob and Timothy Bates found

196
00:13:21,780 --> 00:13:25,640
that pattern in the United States, but
not in Western Europe or Australia.

197
00:13:25,940 --> 00:13:30,320
One reading is that deprivation suppresses
differences that would otherwise show,

198
00:13:30,460 --> 00:13:35,030
and that how strongly it does so depends
on how unequal a society's environments

199
00:13:35,080 --> 00:13:40,650
are. But a large later study of Florida
twins and siblings found no such pattern in

200
00:13:40,700 --> 00:13:44,620
school test scores, so even the
American result is still debated.

201
00:13:45,120 --> 00:13:47,170
Molecular genetics added a twist.

202
00:13:47,220 --> 00:13:52,860
Genome-wide studies find thousands of
genetic variants, each with a tiny effect.

203
00:13:53,580 --> 00:13:58,590
Combined into polygenic scores, the largest
study so far explains about twelve to

204
00:13:58,640 --> 00:14:03,130
sixteen percent of the differences in how
much schooling people complete, well below

205
00:14:03,180 --> 00:14:05,620
twin-study estimates for the same outcome.

206
00:14:05,820 --> 00:14:10,230
And when you compare siblings within the
same family, the score's apparent effect

207
00:14:10,280 --> 00:14:15,050
shrinks by about half. That suggests part
of what the scores capture comes from

208
00:14:15,100 --> 00:14:19,730
family background, such as the environment
parents create and who partners with

209
00:14:19,780 --> 00:14:23,320
whom, rather than from a person's
own genes acting directly.

210
00:14:23,760 --> 00:14:26,880
Meanwhile, the environment
demonstrably moves scores.

211
00:14:27,220 --> 00:14:32,790
A two thousand eighteen meta-analysis by
Stuart Ritchie and Tucker-Drob, drawing on

212
00:14:32,840 --> 00:14:37,710
natural experiments such as changes in the
school-leaving age, estimated that each

213
00:14:37,760 --> 00:14:42,020
extra year of education raises
IQ by roughly one to five points.

214
00:14:42,320 --> 00:14:46,810
High heritability and big
environmental effects at the same time.

215
00:14:46,860 --> 00:14:48,860
That sounds like a contradiction.

216
00:14:49,300 --> 00:14:52,240
It is not one, and the
next finding shows why.

217
00:14:53,140 --> 00:14:58,930
The political scientist James Flynn
documented that average IQ scores rose by

218
00:14:58,980 --> 00:15:03,250
roughly three points per decade through
much of the twentieth century, in many

219
00:15:03,300 --> 00:15:08,740
countries. The gains were largest on abstract
reasoning tests like Raven's matrices,

220
00:15:09,160 --> 00:15:11,740
and smallest on vocabulary and arithmetic.

221
00:15:12,200 --> 00:15:14,860
That is far too fast for genetic change.

222
00:15:15,280 --> 00:15:19,750
Candidate causes include better nutrition
and health, more schooling, smaller

223
00:15:19,800 --> 00:15:24,710
families, and what Flynn called
scientific spectacles, a cultural habit of

224
00:15:24,760 --> 00:15:26,780
classifying and reasoning abstractly.

225
00:15:27,200 --> 00:15:31,130
He liked to cite interviews from the
nineteen thirties in which the psychologist

226
00:15:31,180 --> 00:15:36,870
Alexander Luria posed simple syllogisms
to unschooled farmers in Central Asia, who

227
00:15:36,920 --> 00:15:40,480
often declined to reason about
premises outside their own experience.

228
00:15:40,880 --> 00:15:43,060
Here measurement invariance comes back.

229
00:15:43,280 --> 00:15:48,830
In two thousand four, Jelte Wicherts and
colleagues tested five data sets, comparing

230
00:15:48,880 --> 00:15:53,380
cohorts ten to sixty years apart, and found
that invariance failed in all of them.

231
00:15:53,580 --> 00:15:58,650
The tests were not measuring quite the
same thing in earlier and later cohorts, so

232
00:15:58,700 --> 00:16:02,640
the gains cannot simply be read as
gains in the underlying ability.

233
00:16:02,840 --> 00:16:05,370
And in some countries
the rise has reversed.

234
00:16:05,420 --> 00:16:10,558
Bernt Bratsberg and Ole Rogeberg analyzed
the scores of about seven hundred and

235
00:16:10,608 --> 00:16:12,600
thirty thousand Norwegian conscripts.

236
00:16:13,100 --> 00:16:16,960
Scores rose for men born up to the
mid nineteen seventies and then fell.

237
00:16:17,560 --> 00:16:22,330
Crucially, the decline shows up between
brothers in the same family, which rules out

238
00:16:22,380 --> 00:16:26,370
explanations based on which families
have more children, and points to the

239
00:16:26,420 --> 00:16:29,660
environment. That resolves
the apparent paradox.

240
00:16:30,200 --> 00:16:34,140
Heritability describes variation
within a population at one time.

241
00:16:34,520 --> 00:16:38,480
The Flynn effect is a shift of
the whole population across time.

242
00:16:38,760 --> 00:16:43,470
A trait can be highly heritable and still
move a lot when everyone's environment

243
00:16:43,520 --> 00:16:49,190
changes. That logic carries straight into
the most contentious topic in this field:

244
00:16:49,240 --> 00:16:51,390
average differences between groups.

245
00:16:51,440 --> 00:16:56,690
The key principle is that heritability
within groups does not, by itself, tell you

246
00:16:56,740 --> 00:16:58,560
the causes of differences between groups.

247
00:16:58,860 --> 00:17:02,820
The geneticist Richard Lewontin's
illustration is still the clearest.

248
00:17:03,260 --> 00:17:08,150
Take a bag of genetically varied seed,
and plant one handful in rich soil and

249
00:17:08,200 --> 00:17:12,670
another in poor soil. Within each field,
differences in height may be largely

250
00:17:12,720 --> 00:17:17,450
genetic. But because both handfuls came
from the same bag, the difference between

251
00:17:17,500 --> 00:17:19,840
the fields is entirely environmental.

252
00:17:20,800 --> 00:17:25,330
The analogy shows that high heritability
within groups is compatible with a wholly

253
00:17:25,380 --> 00:17:27,140
environmental gap between them.

254
00:17:27,540 --> 00:17:32,600
By itself, it cannot tell you which
situation holds for any real human groups.

255
00:17:33,020 --> 00:17:36,220
On the evidence itself,
the record runs like this.

256
00:17:36,380 --> 00:17:41,730
After The Bell Curve reignited public
argument in nineteen ninety four, the American

257
00:17:41,780 --> 00:17:45,280
Psychological Association
convened a task force.

258
00:17:45,720 --> 00:17:49,910
Its nineteen ninety six report concluded
that test scores predict important

259
00:17:49,960 --> 00:17:54,160
outcomes. It found that the average
difference between Black and White Americans,

260
00:17:54,360 --> 00:17:59,350
then about one standard deviation, did not
come from obvious bias in the tests and

261
00:17:59,400 --> 00:18:01,420
did not simply reflect social class.

262
00:18:02,280 --> 00:18:07,290
Explanations based on culture and social
position might be right, it said, but had

263
00:18:07,340 --> 00:18:11,330
little direct support, and there was
no direct support at all for a genetic

264
00:18:11,380 --> 00:18:15,460
explanation. Its bottom line
was that no one knew the cause.

265
00:18:15,860 --> 00:18:18,240
Here measurement invariance matters again.

266
00:18:18,580 --> 00:18:23,610
Unlike the gains across generations,
studies comparing Black and White Americans

267
00:18:23,660 --> 00:18:27,860
have mostly found that the tests measure
the same abilities in both groups.

268
00:18:28,000 --> 00:18:31,100
So the gap is not simply
an artifact of the tests.

269
00:18:31,320 --> 00:18:34,420
That tells you what the
gap is, not what causes it.

270
00:18:34,600 --> 00:18:40,170
In two thousand twelve, a review by Richard
Nisbett, Flynn, Turkheimer and others

271
00:18:40,220 --> 00:18:44,030
reported that the gap had narrowed by
about a third of a standard deviation in

272
00:18:44,080 --> 00:18:48,750
recent decades, and its authors argued
that group differences in IQ are best

273
00:18:48,800 --> 00:18:51,020
understood as environmental in origin.

274
00:18:51,560 --> 00:18:57,250
Others, such as Charles Murray, accepted
some narrowing over the century but argued

275
00:18:57,300 --> 00:18:59,690
that it had stalled by the
late nineteen eighties.

276
00:18:59,740 --> 00:19:04,850
And some researchers, most prominently
Arthur Jensen and Philippe Rushton, argued

277
00:19:04,900 --> 00:19:09,110
that genes explain part of the gap,
pointing to indirect evidence such as

278
00:19:09,160 --> 00:19:11,060
heritability and adoption studies.

279
00:19:11,600 --> 00:19:16,670
Critics reply that indirect evidence of
that kind cannot separate genetic from

280
00:19:16,720 --> 00:19:22,110
environmental causes. Opinion among
researchers has been divided, and no direct

281
00:19:22,160 --> 00:19:23,820
evidence has settled the question.

282
00:19:24,300 --> 00:19:26,940
Today's genetic tools
cannot settle it either.

283
00:19:27,680 --> 00:19:32,990
Polygenic scores, built mostly from people
of European ancestry, lose much of their

284
00:19:33,040 --> 00:19:38,090
accuracy in other populations, and even
within one population they partly pick up

285
00:19:38,140 --> 00:19:42,260
family environment. International
comparisons have a further problem.

286
00:19:42,720 --> 00:19:48,130
A widely cited set of national IQ
estimates, compiled by Richard Lynn and Tatu

287
00:19:48,180 --> 00:19:52,890
Vanhanen, rests in many countries on
one or two small samples that were never

288
00:19:52,940 --> 00:19:54,840
designed to represent the whole country.

289
00:19:55,540 --> 00:19:59,790
In two thousand ten, Wicherts and
colleagues reviewed the African data

290
00:19:59,840 --> 00:20:03,680
systematically, with explicit
rules for which samples to include.

291
00:20:03,880 --> 00:20:08,310
Their average came out roughly twelve points
higher than Lynn's, though still below

292
00:20:08,360 --> 00:20:13,650
British norms. They warned that Lynn's
figures rested on unsystematic choices of

293
00:20:13,700 --> 00:20:17,830
samples, and that whether the tests
measure the same thing in those populations

294
00:20:17,880 --> 00:20:19,720
still needed proper study.

295
00:20:19,960 --> 00:20:24,510
Papers built on the dataset are now being
reexamined, and at least three have been

296
00:20:24,560 --> 00:20:29,110
retracted, most recently one in a Royal
Society journal in two thousand twenty four

297
00:20:29,160 --> 00:20:32,090
and another in two thousand twenty five.

298
00:20:32,140 --> 00:20:35,110
Countries really do differ on
international school assessments.

299
00:20:35,160 --> 00:20:40,090
But how much of that reflects the same
underlying ability, and why the differences

300
00:20:40,140 --> 00:20:42,020
exist, are separate questions.

301
00:20:42,380 --> 00:20:47,220
And the Flynn effect is a reminder that
national averages move as countries develop.

302
00:20:48,530 --> 00:20:50,580
Two neighboring ideas, briefly.

303
00:20:50,900 --> 00:20:56,230
Howard Gardner's multiple intelligences
are hugely popular in education, but when

304
00:20:56,280 --> 00:21:00,730
researchers built measures of the supposedly
separate intelligences, most of them

305
00:21:00,780 --> 00:21:02,800
loaded on g and on each other.

306
00:21:03,520 --> 00:21:05,760
Rationality is the more interesting case.

307
00:21:06,000 --> 00:21:11,090
The psychologist Keith Stanovich coined
the word dysrationalia for smart people who

308
00:21:11,140 --> 00:21:16,170
reason badly. Resisting a tempting but
wrong intuition turns out to be at least

309
00:21:16,220 --> 00:21:21,290
partly a different skill from raw reasoning
power, though the two are related, and

310
00:21:21,340 --> 00:21:23,780
standard intelligence
tests largely miss it.

311
00:21:25,030 --> 00:21:30,130
So if someone tells me a language model
has an IQ of a hundred and thirty, what

312
00:21:30,180 --> 00:21:33,720
should I ask? First, what the
number is supposed to measure.

313
00:21:34,160 --> 00:21:38,990
Human abilities really do covary, and
the general factor that summarizes them

314
00:21:39,040 --> 00:21:44,430
predicts real outcomes, but at least four
explanations fit the same data, and the

315
00:21:44,480 --> 00:21:47,140
factor's biological meaning is still open.

316
00:21:47,340 --> 00:21:51,630
Then ask whether the test means the same
thing for that system as for the people it

317
00:21:51,680 --> 00:21:55,240
was built for, which is the
invariance question in a new setting.

318
00:21:55,440 --> 00:21:59,540
And keep in mind how easily the
numbers people fight about are misread.

319
00:22:00,280 --> 00:22:03,060
Heritability describes
populations, not persons.

320
00:22:03,420 --> 00:22:06,460
It coexists with large
environmental effects.

321
00:22:06,720 --> 00:22:10,300
And by itself it cannot explain
differences between groups.

322
00:22:10,600 --> 00:22:14,890
Most of all, notice that everything in
this episode has been about how humans

323
00:22:14,940 --> 00:22:16,180
compare with other humans.

324
00:22:16,460 --> 00:22:21,730
A test built for schoolchildren tells you
little, by itself, about what a crow, an

325
00:22:21,780 --> 00:22:24,520
octopus or a language model has or lacks.

326
00:22:24,880 --> 00:22:27,250
For that, we leave the human case behind.

327
00:22:27,300 --> 00:22:33,150
Next episode: minds in other bodies, from
a crow that bent a wire into a hook to

328
00:22:33,200 --> 00:22:35,900
cells that solve problems
without any brain at all.
